.TH RELIS 1 "2026-08-01" "scheduler" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
relis \- proofread the files this ecosystem writes about itself
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B relis list
.br
.B relis all
.br
.B relis blockers
.br
.B relis questions
.br
.B relis replies
.br
.B relis rotation
.br
.B relis witness
.br
.B relis
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B relis
proofreads the files this ecosystem writes about itself. Every check it fronts
reads one such file \- a registry conf, a QUESTIONS.md, a BLOCKERS.md, a
reply, a rotation \- and reports where it no longer holds the shape its own
consumers assume. None of them dispatch anything and none of them spend.
.PP
.B Why it is not part of
.BR dose .
.B dose
discovered twenty\-five subcommands from real tooling and welded four domains
into one table: dispatch, reporting, usage\-gating, and these five checks. A
caller reaching for a lint and a caller reaching for the crontab generator want
different things, for different reasons, and pipe the answers somewhere
different. One noun carries many verbs; this is the seam with the clearest
edge.
.SS It wraps, it does not reimplement
Every check is an existing script in scheduler's own
.IR bin/ ,
executed unchanged. A wrapper that quietly reimplements what it fronts is two
implementations that drift apart, and the drift is discovered by whichever one
a caller happened to use.
.TP
.B blockers
freshness of BLOCKERS.md entries.
.TP
.B questions
whether QUESTIONS.md entries carry what an answer binds to.
.TP
.B replies
whether a `> ` answer is shaped the way its reader parses.
.TP
.B rotation
whether the rotation files agree about who dispatches what.
.TP
.B witness
whether a check that claims a witness left one behind.
.SS all
Runs every check.
.B The worst outcome wins, never the last one.
A runner that returns its final command's status reports clean whenever the
cleanest check happens to run last, and a check that could not run at all
outranks findings \- a pass on the four that ran does not establish the fifth.
.SS Findings are exit 7, always
The wrapped scripts return 1 and 2 of their own. Passing those through
unchanged makes a file that lost its shape indistinguishable from a mistyped
argument, because 2 already means the caller is wrong here \- and 1 is
reserved by the shared vocabulary. A finding about the SUBJECT gets a code
declared above that vocabulary, which is the same reason
.BR bashify (1)
returns 7 when the page it tested failed a row. The subject failing is not the
tool failing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Not wired for any subcommand of this verb. Requesting it is reported rather
than ignored.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress commentary; results only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every check that ran found nothing.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: a check could not run because the script it fronts is absent or not
executable. It outranks a finding, because the domain it covers went unread.
.TP
.B 7
At least one file has lost its shape. Declared above the shared vocabulary
rather than reusing one of its codes, because the subject failing is not the
tool failing.
.SH EXAMPLES
Listing prints the subcommands, bare, one per line, so a caller reads the
surface without a parser:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ relis list
list
all
blockers
questions
replies
rotation
witness
.fi
.RE
.PP
An unknown subcommand is the caller's error, not a finding:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ relis frobnicate
relis: unknown subcommand: frobnicate  (try: relis list)
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.SH COST
Nothing here spends money. This utility reaches no paid service, and it offers
no
.B \-\-summon
flag for any subcommand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/relis
This utility.
.TP
.I lib/verb.sh
The shared runtime it sources.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dose (1),
.BR arme (1),
.BR lint (1),
.BR make (1)
.PP
Expectations transfer from
.BR lint (1)
for what a proofreader does, and from
.BR dose (1)
for the domain this verb was cut out of.
